/-!
# Parabolic Shapiro lifting for Gamma1(N) in every coefficient degree

Theorem statement: `MTT.Cohomology.exists_parabolic_coinduced_cocycle`
(`96bbed5a-210d-4d60-8934-d83a912e2875`), by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-07.

Proof: submission `aef03d31-6fe8-4eaa-bbfb-2f0d979ac951`, by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-07 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

Let $N>0$ and $n\ge0$. Put $H=\Gamma_1(N)$, $G=\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$, and
$V=\operatorname{Sym}^{n}(\mathbb C^2)$ with the MTT left action. Every parabolic one-cocycle
$b:H\to V$ admits a one-cocycle $C:G\to\operatorname{Coind}_H^G V$ satisfying $C(h)(1)=b(h)$ for all
$h\in H$. The lift is parabolic: for every rational cusp $x$ and every $g\in G$ fixing $x$, there is
a coinduced vector $P$ such that $C(g)=gP-P$. This supplies the cocycle-level parabolic
compatibility needed to transport the MTT dimension problem to the full modular group. It includes
degree zero and all positive levels.

## Explanation of the source proof

# Parabolic Shapiro lifting

Write $H=\Gamma_1(N)$ and $G=\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$. Choose a right
transversal containing $1$, and write $x=r(x)t(x)$ with $r(x)\in H$ and
$t(x)$ in the transversal. Thus $r(hx)=hr(x)$ and $r(h)=h$ for $h\in H$.
For a cocycle $b:H\to V$, define
$$C(g)(x)=b(r(xg))-b(r(x)).$$
The cocycle identity for $b$ proves left $H$-equivariance in $x$. Telescoping
proves the $G$-cocycle identity for $C$, where coinduction acts by right
translation. Evaluation at $1$ recovers $b$ exactly.

Now let $g$ fix a rational cusp $y$. The normal finite-index subgroup
$\Gamma(N)\subseteq H$ supplies a positive integer $m=[G:\Gamma(N)]$
such that $g^m\in\Gamma(N)$. Every transversal conjugate
$t g^m t^{-1}$ lies in $H$ and fixes $t y$. Parabolicity of $b$ therefore
makes its value on each such conjugate principal. Restriction to the
transversal is a linear equivalence from the coinduced module to functions
on that transversal; the chosen principal witnesses assemble into one
coinduced vector $F$ with
$$C(g^m)=(g^m-1)F.$$

Finally, pass to the coinvariants $V'/(g-1)V'$ of the coinduced module $V'$.
There $g$ acts trivially, so the cocycle identity gives
$[C(g^m)]=m[C(g)]$. The left-hand side vanishes by the displayed principal
identity. Since $m$ is nonzero in characteristic zero, $[C(g)]=0$, proving
$C(g)\in(g-1)V'$. This works for every rational cusp and every element
fixing it, not only for the translation at infinity.

The source statement is parabolic Shapiro in Columbia's Spring 2021
Eichler-Shimura seminar notes, Section 1.1, Lemma 1, page 2. The finite-power
argument adapts the existing proved Gamma0 result
p2m:theorem/3b95e6da-4049-5a35-810c-3de89df0e00e, specifically proof
p2m:solution/c7770f6c-0e05-5212-ac9a-be8ca5205c57. The transversal lift also
matches the algebraic part of
p2m:solution/4daf947c-3676-51e9-8e78-97c720db5c26. Both originate in the
FLT repository at aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef. The current proof
uses mathlib's coinduced representation and MTT's rational-cusp condition;
the cyclic power argument is proved directly through coinvariants.

All needed algebra is reproved in the submitted file. It imports no platform
theorem, only mathlib and the frozen MTT definition. The exact standalone
solution passes a guarded axiom check with only propext, Classical.choice,
and Quot.sound. There is no sorry, custom axiom, native_decide, or raised
resource limit. This establishes the lift and its parabolic compatibility,
not the remaining analytic cusp-form dimension comparison.

